# Lesson 3, new exercise: trumps missing the ace and king. Lead them anyway: those two losers can't be avoided.
title: Don't be afraid to lose trumps
id: L03-17
type: exercise
lesson: 3
concepts: 3.3, 3.4
student: S
board: 59
dealer: S
vulnerable: none

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  K82    7654   Q4     AK65
  E  7653   A2     T763   874
  S  A4     QJT98  AK52   32
  W  QJT9   K3     J98    QJT9

auction:
  S 1H*    = 13+ points, at least four hearts
     not *: 14 high-card points and five hearts: what does the lesson open?
  W Pass
  N 3H     = 4+ hearts, an opening hand or better: game forcing
  E Pass
  S 4H*    = Game
     key: Partner's jump raise forces to game. Bid it.
     not Pass: Partner's 3♥ forces to game. Did you add 16 and 12?
     not *: Partner showed an opening hand or better and four hearts. Can you see 25?
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1♥ and partner's jump to 3♥ showed four hearts and an opening hand or
  better, so you bid game. You are declarer, because you bid hearts first,
  and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 4
  losers: 2
  spades: 0 = The ace and king.
  hearts: 2 = The ace and king. Lose them early.
  diamonds: 0 = The fourth diamond, but dummy can ruff it.
  clubs: 0 = The ace and king.
  remind: You can afford three losers in 4♥. Count the obvious losers suit by suit in your own hand.
  remind: Two of your losers are in trumps. When should you lose them?
play:
  1   W SQ  N S2  E S3  S SA
      note: Three losers: the ♥A, the ♥K and the fourth diamond. You can afford all three. The trump losers can't be avoided, so lose them now, before the opponents can ruff your winners.
  2   S HQ  W H3  N H4  E HA
      note: Lead trumps, even though you're missing the ace and king. They will win tricks sooner or later, so let them win now, while you still control the other suits.
  3   E S5  S S4  W ST  N SK
  4   N H5  E H2  S HJ  W HK
      note: Keep leading trumps. This time West wins the king, and the opponents have none left.
  5   W SJ  N S8  E S6  S H8*
      H8 key: You have no spades left, so ruff.
      H8 not *: Did you notice that you have no spades left? Ruff West's winner.
      note: Ruff. Now take your winners.
  6   S DA  W D8  N D4  E D3
      note: The diamonds: ace, then over to dummy's queen.
  7   S D5  W D9  N DQ  E D6
      note: Over to dummy's ♦Q.
  8   N CA  E C4  S C2  W C9
      note: Dummy's clubs.
  9   N CK  E C7  S C3  W CT
      note: The ♣K.
  10  N C6  E C8  S H9  W CJ
      note: Ruff to get back to your hand.
  11  S DK  W DJ  N C5  E D7
      note: The ♦K.
  12  S D2  W S9  N H6  E DT
      note: Dummy ruffs your last diamond.
  13  N H7  E S7  S HT  W CQ
      note: 11 tricks: 4♥ made with an overtrick.

play recap:
  The plan: count three losers, two of them in trumps, and lose the trump
  tricks first.

  You lost the lead twice, early on, and nothing bad happened. Losing the
  tricks you must lose, at a time of your choosing, is a big part of
  declarer play.
